KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ tail reused: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ distinct bytes: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ dsize\(O\): SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ POD member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The expected output is that of g++ 13 and clang++ 18.  CBMC lays out a
non-empty [[no_unique_address]] member as any other member: sizeof(P) is
12 and P::c is at offset 8, and offsetof(first_padding_byte<O>, ...) is 8,
so the program fails the first and third assertions.

Why FUTURE and not KNOWNBUG: CBMC's layout conforms to N5008.  Whether a
potentially-overlapping subobject overlaps anything is
implementation-defined ([intro.object]/8; [dcl.attr.nouniqueaddr]/2 is a
note saying the padding "can" be reused), and every object here keeps
storage disjoint from every other ([intro.object]/10).  What CBMC does not
match is the Itanium C++ ABI that GCC and Clang implement.

What it would take: CBMC's struct type has no notion of two components
sharing bytes, so reusing the tail padding needs P::c to sit inside
the bytes of P::o.  The empty-member support (cpp_typecheck_compound_type,
make_empty_potentially_overlapping_members_zero_sized) avoids that by
giving the member no bytes and reaching it through `*(T *)((char *)&x +
offset)'; a non-empty member has bytes, so either the member would be
cut to its dsize (an array of dsize bytes, reached the same way, with its
tail padding left to the following members) or the following members
would be reached at their ABI offsets.  dsize itself would have to be
computed per class (the size without tail padding, Itanium 2.4 II).
Also needed for base classes: CBMC already gives D : B (B non-POD with
tail padding) the ABI size, by flattening.

Consequences of the gap: sizeof and offsetof of such classes differ from
GCC and Clang, and libc++'s __compressed_pair (Xcode 26) pads a stateful
element of such a type differently; that changes layouts, not results.
